('eeae7d42-d98e-592a-be19-213c0adbc6ec', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', 'a3159ca9-af9c-407a-9d2b-1f70f0f4bf7b', 'a-level', '18.2', '9702 coursebook 18.2 · Observing oscillations', 21, 21, 'Which measurement shows that an oscillation is isochronous (its period does not depend on amplitude)?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The time for 10 oscillations is the same for small and for larger releases', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Isochronous means equal times: timing the same number of swings at different amplitudes and finding no change. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (The time for 10 oscillations is the same for small and for larger releases).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('23048ef7-e494-5761-b2f6-4ef7e1c41523', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', 'a3159ca9-af9c-407a-9d2b-1f70f0f4bf7b', 'a-level', '18.2', '9702 coursebook 18.2 · Observing oscillations', 22, 22, 'A metronome is set to 120 beats per minute, with one beat at each end of its swing. Calculate the period of its oscillation.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Two beats per oscillation: 120 beats per minute = 60 oscillations per minute, so T = 60 ÷ 60 = 1.0 s. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: two beats per cycle. A1: T = 1.0 s.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('6dfa6a2a-a26d-5069-a4ee-5978aadc385e', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', 'a3159ca9-af9c-407a-9d2b-1f70f0f4bf7b', 'a-level', '18.2', '9702 coursebook 18.2 · Observing oscillations', 23, 23, 'A student times 20 oscillations of a pendulum as 31.6 s, with an uncertainty of ±0.2 s in each timing. Calculate the period and its percentage uncertainty. Give the percentage uncertainty.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.6329', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 31.6 ÷ 20 = 1.58 s. Absolute uncertainty in T = 0.2 ÷ 20 = 0.01 s. Percentage = 0.01 ÷ 1.58 × 100 = 0.63 %. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = t/20. C1: uncertainty divided by 20. C1: percentage.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('361df53f-fdb9-5068-ba47-d2a1d47b742f', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', 'a3159ca9-af9c-407a-9d2b-1f70f0f4bf7b', 'a-level', '18.2', '9702 coursebook 18.2 · Observing oscillations', 24, 24, 'An oscilloscope shows a sinusoidal trace with peak-to-peak height 5.6 divisions on a setting of 0.50 cm per division (the trace shows displacement of a vibrating plate). One cycle is 3.2 divisions wide at 5.0 ms per division. Calculate the amplitude of the plate in cm.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.4 cm', 'cm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Peak-to-peak = 5.6 × 0.50 = 2.8 cm, so amplitude = 2.8 ÷ 2 = 1.4 cm. (Period = 3.2 × 5.0 = 16 ms.). Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: peak-to-peak × scale. C1: halve for amplitude. B1: period read correctly.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 380 338" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgb66764e8-t fgb66764e8-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgb66764e8-t">Oscilloscope trace</title><desc id="fgb66764e8-d">An oscilloscope trace of a vibrating plate: peak-to-peak height 5.6 divisions at 0.50 cm per division; one cycle is 3.2 divisions wide at 5.0 ms per division.</desc><rect x="20" y="12" width="340" height="272" rx="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="54" y1="12" x2="54" y2="284" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="88" y1="12" x2="88" y2="284" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="122" y1="12" x2="122" y2="284" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="156" y1="12" x2="156" y2="284" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x