('4730137a-b1b0-557c-b58e-fc1d5cdb8815', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 13, 13, 'Two oscillators of frequency 5.0 Hz are out of step by 0.050 s. Calculate their phase difference in radians.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.571', 'rad', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'phase difference = 2π × Δt/T = 2π × 0.050 × 5.0 = 1.57 rad. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: φ = 2πΔt/T. A1: φ = 1.57 rad.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('7c8e043d-7c08-5627-975e-e246381e981b', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 14, 14, 'Two identical masses on springs oscillate in antiphase. Which statement is correct?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'When one is at its highest point, the other is at its lowest point', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Antiphase means a phase difference of π rad: one is at +A when the other is at −A. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (When one is at its highest point, the other is at its lowest point).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('791c3584-63e7-5c4c-a75b-18c6705bfecc', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 15, 15, 'A spinning wheel has a valve that goes up and down 3.5 times per second. Calculate the period and the angular frequency of this vertical oscillation. Give the angular frequency.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '21.99', 'rad s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ω = 2πf = 2π × 3.5 = 22.0 rad s⁻¹ (T = 1/3.5 = 0.29 s). Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: ω = 2πf. A1: ω = 22.0 rad s⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('d993274a-9481-5ebb-8295-7e7edbd8e8fc', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 16, 16, 'An oscillation has angular frequency 50 rad s⁻¹. Calculate its period.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.1257', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2π/ω = 2π ÷ 50 = 0.126 s. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: T = 2π/ω. A1: T = 0.126 s.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('c9c6738d-d4bc-5cd5-82cb-ce38ff85b0cc', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 17, 17, 'Which quantity is the maximum displacement of an oscillating object from its equilibrium position?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Amplitude', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The amplitude is the maximum displacement; the peak-to-peak distance is twice the amplitude. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (Amplitude).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('a0256289-436b-5b70-af49-daa460369589', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 18, 18, 'Two oscillations have a phase difference of 2π rad. How do they compare?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They are in phase', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '2π rad is one whole cycle, so the two are exactly in step: in phase. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (They are in phase).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('c0df6d0f-da9e-5b42-aaa1-28def66643ea', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 19, 19, 'Express a phase difference of 135° in radians.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.356', 'rad', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'φ = 135 × π ÷ 180 = 2.36 rad (3π/4). Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: × π/180. A1: φ = 2.36 rad.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('974d40ec-286e-5894-a83f-ad9bb1a8b7a4', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 20, 20, 'An electrical signal on an oscilloscope repeats every 2.5 ms. What is its frequency?', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '400', 'Hz', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'f = 1/T = 1 ÷ 2.5 × 10⁻³ = 400 Hz. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: f = 400 Hz.', 1, NULL, NULL),
('8413c6b2-c6ee-5982-90ee-bd11dc269fb1', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 21, 21, 'A swing has a period of 3.0 s. A second swing starts 1.5 s later and has the same period. What is their phase difference?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'π rad', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '1.5 s is half a period, which corresponds to π rad. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (π rad).', 1, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fga95efbd6-t fga95efbd6-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fga95efbd6-t">Two swings</title><desc id="fga95efbd6-d">Displacement against time: swing A starts at t = 0 with period 3.0 s; swing B (dashed) starts 1.5 s later with the same period.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="113.5" y1="34" x2="113.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="203" y1="34" x2="203" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="292.5" y1="34" x2="292.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="148.8" x2="382" y2="148.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="148.8" x2="388" y2="148.8" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejo