or two pendulums of period 1.60 s, each released from its maximum displacement: B (dashed) is released 0.20 s after A.'),
('5f8e5453-1ff2-5bd4-abf1-e3241b734d7c', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 24, 24, 'Oscillator P passes through its equilibrium position moving upwards at the instant oscillator Q (same frequency) is at its highest point. What is the phase difference?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'π/2 rad, with Q a quarter cycle ahead', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Q reached the equilibrium position moving upward a quarter cycle earlier, so Q leads P by π/2 rad. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: quarter of a cycle. C1: π/2 rad. A1: C (π/2 rad, with Q a quarter cycle ahead).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('4b6c147f-a857-532a-a2ad-278157023a73', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 25, 25, 'A displacement–time graph of a tuning-fork prong has 12 complete cycles in 25 ms. Calculate (a) the period and (b) the angular frequency. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3016', 'rad s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) T = 25 ÷ 12 = 2.08 ms. (b) ω = 2π/T = 2π ÷ 0.002083 = 3020 rad s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = t/N. C1: ω = 2π/T. C1: ms to s.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('f6dfba69-b24f-5a80-addc-c8f7e15c1780', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 26, 26, 'Two masses oscillate with the same period of 0.80 s and a phase difference of 3π/2 rad. Calculate the time interval between them passing through their equilibrium positions in the same direction.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.6', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Fraction of cycle = (3π/2) ÷ 2π = 0.75. Δt = 0.75 × 0.80 = 0.60 s (equivalently, one leads the other by 0.20 s). Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: fraction = φ/2π. C1: Δt = fraction × T. A1: Δt = 0.60 s.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('73b6a2b6-7905-5dc1-baa6-46027747e7fa', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 27, 27, 'A vibrating string has amplitude 2.0 mm and frequency 440 Hz. What total distance does a point on the string travel in one second?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '3.52 m', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'In each cycle it travels 4A = 8.0 mm. In one second: 440 × 8.0 mm = 3520 mm = 3.52 m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: distance per cycle = 4A. C1: × f. A1: A (3.52 m).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('5a9c336a-c817-593d-80d7-c680326d40f6', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 28, 28, 'A pendulum has amplitude 5.0 cm and period 2.0 s. Calculate the total distance travelled by the bob in 10 s.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'In 10 s it makes 10 ÷ 2.0 = 5 complete oscillations, each covering 4A = 0.20 m. Distance = 5 × 0.20 = 1.0 m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: number of oscillations. C1: 4A per cycle. A1: distance = 1.0 m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ca2aed13-7441-5f09-afa0-82a5ca0a1db4', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 29, 29, 'Two oscillators have frequencies of 2.0 Hz and 2.1 Hz and start in phase. After how long are they next in phase again?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '10 s', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The faster one gains 0.1 cycles per second, so it gains one whole cycle (back in phase) after 1 ÷ 0.1 = 10 s. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: difference in frequency. C1: gain one cycle. C1: t = 1/Δf. A1: D (10 s).', 4, NULL, NULL),
('659fa18a-f1e4-529a-83b2-6839dcad1f07', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 30, 30, 'A phase difference of 0.75 rad exists between two oscillations of frequency 12 Hz. Calculate the time interval this represents.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.009947', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Δt = (φ/2π) × T = (0.75 ÷ 2π) × (1 ÷ 12) = 0.00995 s. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = 1/f. C1: Δt = φT/2π. A1: Δt = 0.00995 s.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('42c6da3b-56ca-5ccf-a9ce-1d0f5baf30d4', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 31, 31, 'Which statement about two oscillations with a phase difference of π rad and equal amplitudes is correct?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'If they are added (superposed), they cancel completely', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'At every instant one is +x and the other −x, so the sum is zero: complete cancellation. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: antiphase. C1: x₁ = −x₂ at every instant. A1: B (If they are added (superposed), they cancel completely).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('5c21f639-4850-5179-bb84-7fe437951c8b', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 32, 32, 'An oscillator obeys x = 0.12 cos(5.0t) in SI units. State (a) the amplitude, (b) the angular frequency, and calculate (c) the period. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.257', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 0.12 m. (b) 5.0 rad s⁻¹. (c) T = 2π/ω = 2π ÷ 5.0 = 1.26 s. Mark scheme (4 marks). B1: amplitude. B1: ω. C1: T = 2π/ω. A1: T = 1.26 s.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('0b9194b8-450e-595d-b04a-79c91171298e', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 33, 33, 'A tuning fork of frequency 512 Hz vibrates with amplitude 0.40 mm. Calculate the time it takes a prong to move from one extreme position to the other.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.766e-4', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Extreme to extreme is half a period: t = T/2 = 1 ÷ (2 × 512) = 9.77 × 10⁻⁴ s. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = 1/f. C1: half period. A1: t = 9.77 × 10⁻⁴ s.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('f9d72a31-728e-5b8a-8f77-c95e60d6929e', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 34, 34, 'Two pendulums start swinging together, in phase. Pendulum A has period 1.0 s and B has period 1.2 s. What is their phase relationship at t = 3.0 s?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Antiphase', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'At 3.0 s, A has made 3 cycles and B has made 2.5. The difference is half a cycle: antiphase. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: cycles completed by each. C1: difference of ½ cycle. A1: C (Antiphase).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('8a0d7786-f392-5640-b84a-9a18e00d6817', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 35, 35, 'Two points on a vibrating string are 0.25 cycles out of step. The string vibrates at 200 Hz. Calculate the time difference between them reaching maximum displacement.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.00125', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Δt = fraction × T = 0.25 × (1 ÷ 200) = 1.25 × 10⁻³ s. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T = 1/f. C1: Δt = 0.25T. A1: Δt = 0.00125 s.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('8319511e-d719-55ce-8d0a-1793d5d8785b', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 36, 36, 'An object oscillates with x = 4.0 sin(3πt) cm, with t in s. Calculate its frequency and the first time after t = 0 at which x = 4.0 cm. Give that time.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.1667', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ω = 3π rad s⁻¹, so f = ω/2π = 1.5 Hz. x = 4.0 cm when sin(3πt) = 1, first at 3πt = π/2, so t = 1 ÷ 6 = 0.167 s. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: ω = 3π. C1: f = 1.5 Hz. C1: sin = 1 at π/2. A1: t = 0.167 s.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg094a0893-t fg094a0893-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg094a0893-t">x = 4.0 sin(3πt)</title><desc id="fg094a0893-d">Graph of x = 4.0 sin(3πt) cm against time from 0 to 4/3 s: two complete cycles of amplitude 4.0 cm.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="82.3" y1="34" x2="82.3" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="135.2" y1="34" x2="135.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="188.1" y1="34" x2="188.1" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="241" y1="34" x2="241" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="293.9" y1="34" x2="293.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="346.7"