rrentColor">B</text></svg>', 'Displacement against time for oscillations A and B of period 8.0 ms: each peak of B (dashed) comes 2.0 ms after the matching peak of A.'),
('e76a6fa1-1ba1-50df-a401-13becb35303b', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 40, 40, 'A wheel turns at 4.0 rev s⁻¹; the shadow of a peg on its rim (radius 0.15 m) moves back and forth on a wall in simple harmonic motion. Calculate the amplitude, period and angular frequency of the shadow''s motion. Give the angular frequency.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '25.13', 'rad s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Amplitude = radius = 0.15 m. T = 1 ÷ 4.0 = 0.25 s. ω = 2πf = 2π × 4.0 = 25.1 rad s⁻¹. Mark scheme (4 marks). B1: amplitude = radius. C1: T = 1/f. C1: ω = 2πf. A1: ω = 25.1 rad s⁻¹.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('8951d971-2e98-5abd-af1c-413cd69a57d8', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 41, 41, 'Two simple pendulums have lengths 1.00 m and 0.81 m. They are released together, in phase. (a) Calculate both periods. (b) Calculate how many oscillations the shorter one makes before they are next in phase. (c) Calculate the time at which this happens. Give only the answer to (c). Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '18.05', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) T₁ = 2π × √(1.00 ÷ 9.81) = 2.01 s, T₂ = 2π × √(0.81 ÷ 9.81) = 1.81 s. (b) T ∝ √L: T₂/T₁ = 0.9, so after 10 swings of the short one the long one has made 9: n = 10. (c) t = 10 × 1.805 = 18.1 s. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: T = 2π√(L/g). A1: both periods. M1: ratio 0.9 → gains one swing in 10. A1: 10 oscillations. A1: t = 18.1 s.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('86fdd2f3-8409-5efb-999b-1fe7fe9ccc3a', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 42, 42, 'A displacement–time graph shows two oscillations, X and Y, of period 40 ms. X has amplitude 3.0 mm and a maximum at t = 0. Y has amplitude 2.0 mm and a maximum at t = 30 ms. (a) Calculate the phase difference, stating which leads. (b) Write an equation for each in the form x = A cos(ωt + φ). (c) Calculate the displacement of Y at t = 10 ms. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '-2 mm', 'mm', false, NULL, true, NULL, '(a) 30 ms is 0.75 of a cycle: φ = 3π/2 rad, Y lags X (equivalently Y leads X by π/2). (b) ω = 2π/0.040 = 157 rad s⁻¹. X: x = 3.0 cos(ωt) mm. Y: x = 2.0 cos(ω(t − 0.030)) mm. (c) At t = 10 ms, Y is 20 ms (half a period) before its peak: x = 2.0 × cos(−180°) = 2.0 × cos 180° = −2.0 mm (Y is at its most negative displacement). Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: fraction of period. A1: phase difference and which leads. C1: ω = 2π/T. B1: equations. C1: substitution. A1: x = −2.0 mm.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fge75b11e5-t fge75b11e5-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fge75b11e5-t">Oscillations X and Y</title><desc id="fge75b11e5-d">Displacement against time for X (amplitude 3.0 mm, maximum at t = 0) and Y (dashed; amplitude 2.0 mm, maximum at t = 30 ms), both of period 40 ms.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="73.5" y1="34" x2="73.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="117.6" y1="34" x2="117.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stro