,106 355.9,106.7 356.6,107.5 357.3,108.3 358,109.2 358.7,110 359.4,110.9 360.1,111.9 360.8,112.8 361.6,113.8 362.3,114.8 363,115.8 363.7,116.8 364.4,117.8 365.1,118.9 365.8,120 366.5,121.1 367.2,122.2 367.9,123.3 368.6,124.5 369.3,125.7 370,126.9 370.7,128 371.4,129.3 372.1,130.5 372.8,131.7 373.5,133 374.2,134.2 374.9,135.5 375.7,136.7 376.4,138 377.1,139.3 377.8,140.6 378.5,141.9 379.2,143.2 379.9,144.5 380.6,145.8 381.3,147.1 382,148.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><line x1="278.2" y1="14" x2="300.2" y2="14" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="306.2" y="14" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">X</text><line x1="330.1" y1="14" x2="352.1" y2="14" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><text x="358.1" y="14" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Y</text></svg>', 'Displacement against time for X (amplitude 3.0 mm, maximum at t = 0) and Y (dashed; amplitude 2.0 mm, maximum at t = 30 ms), both of period 40 ms.'),
('0dee361f-4761-5eb2-ad03-c52450746879', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 43, 43, 'A point on the Earth''s Equator is viewed from far above the North Pole. Its shadow on a distant screen (along a line through the Earth''s centre) moves with simple harmonic motion. Earth radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m, rotation period = 24.0 h. (a) State the amplitude. (b) Calculate the angular frequency. (c) Calculate the maximum speed of the shadow. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '463.2', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) A = 6.37 × 10⁶ m (the radius). (b) ω = 2π/T = 2π ÷ 86400 = 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ rad s⁻¹. (c) v_max = ωA = 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ × 6.37 × 10⁶ = 463 m s⁻¹ (equal to the speed of the point itself). Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: amplitude = radius. C1: ω = 2π/T. A1: ω. C1: v_max = ωA. A1: v_max = 463 m s⁻¹.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('1e47796e-fde4-5b03-9b25-e17dd17389bf', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 44, 44, 'Two loudspeakers are driven by the same 680 Hz signal. A microphone is 0.25 m further from one speaker than the other. The speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the wavelength. (b) Calculate the phase difference, in radians, between the two waves at the microphone. (c) State what is heard there and explain. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.142', 'rad', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) λ = v/f = 340 ÷ 680 = 0.50 m. (b) Path difference 0.25 m is half a wavelength: φ = 2π × 0.25 ÷ 0.50 = 3.14 rad. (c) The waves arrive in antiphase, so they cancel (destructive interference): a quiet point. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: λ = v/f. A1: λ. C1: φ = 2π × path difference/λ. A1: φ = 3.14 rad. B1: antiphase. B1: quiet / destructive.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('553628ea-1564-5bac-9c0b-977bbb4d5c69', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 45, 45, 'A car''s wheels are 0.66 m in diameter. The car moves at 14 m s⁻¹. A reflector on the wheel rim moves up and down. (a) Calculate the frequency of this vertical oscillation. (b) State its amplitude. (c) Calculate the angular frequency. Give only the answer to (a).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.752', 'Hz', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) One cycle per revolution: f = v/(2πr) = 14 ÷ (π × 0.66) = 6.75 Hz. (b) A = r = 0.33 m. (c) ω = 2πf = 42.4 rad s⁻¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: one oscillation per revolution. C1: f = v/(2πr). A1: f = 6.75 Hz. B1: A = 0.33 m. B1: ω = 2πf.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('4f8b9dfb-6ae6-50b9-9c00-c8fc89dc8fd0', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 46, 46, 'Oscillator P obeys x_P = 5.0 sin(4πt) cm and Q obeys x_Q = 5.0 sin(4πt + π/3) cm. (a) State their common frequency. (b) State the phase difference and which leads. (c) Calculate the time interval between P and Q reaching their maximum displacement. (d) Calculate x_Q at the instant P is at its maximum. Give only the answer to (d).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.5 cm', 'cm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ω = 4π rad s⁻¹, so f = 2.0 Hz. (b) π/3 rad, Q leads. (c) Δt = (π/3) ÷ (4π) = 1/12 = 0.083 s. (d) P is at its maximum when 4πt = π/2, so x_Q = 5.0 × sin(90° + 60°) = 5.0 × sin 150° = 2.50 cm. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: f = 2.0 Hz. B1: π/3, Q leads. C1: Δt = φ/ω. A1: 0.083 s. C1: phase of Q at P maximum. A1: x_Q = 2.50 cm.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg32012180-t fg32012180-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg32012180-t">Oscillators P and Q</title><desc id="fg32012180-d">Graphs of x_P = 5.0 sin(4πt) cm (solid) and x_Q = 5.0 sin(4πt + π/3) cm (dashed) from t = 0 to 1.0 s.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="117.6" y1="34" x2="117.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.7" y1="34" x2="205.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="293.9" y1="34" x2="293.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x