('7917cdb3-ff8b-52e9-8a4a-7fa3e57834f8', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 47, 47, 'Two children on identical swings are pushed so that they swing with the same period, 3.0 s, but child B passes the lowest point 0.50 s after child A. (a) Calculate the phase difference in radians. (b) State the phase difference in degrees. (c) Calculate how much later B would need to be pushed for them to be in antiphase. Give only the answer to (a).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.047', 'rad', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) φ = 2πΔt/T = 2π × 0.50 ÷ 3.0 = 1.05 rad. (b) 0.50 ÷ 3.0 × 360 = 60°. (c) Antiphase needs Δt = T/2 = 1.5 s. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: φ = 2πΔt/T. A1: φ = 1.05 rad. B1: 60°. C1: T/2 for antiphase. A1: 1.5 s.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('222cdcbb-25b6-5b25-b1c2-bab4ff1f93e5', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 48, 48, 'A wheel of radius 0.20 m spins at 2.0 rev s⁻¹. A peg on its rim casts a shadow on a vertical wall from a horizontal beam of light, and the shadow moves up and down. (a) Explain why the shadow moves with s.h.m. (b) Calculate the amplitude, period and maximum speed of the shadow. (c) A second peg is 90° further round the rim. State the phase difference between the two shadows. Give only the maximum speed in (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.513', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The shadow''s height is r sin θ with θ = ωt increasing uniformly: y = r sin ωt, which is s.h.m. (b) A = 0.20 m, T = 0.50 s, v_max = ωA = 2π × 2.0 × 0.20 = 2.51 m s⁻¹. (c) π/2 rad. Mark scheme (6 marks). M1: y = r sin ωt. B1: A and T. C1: v_max = ωA. A1: v_max = 2.51 m s⁻¹. B1: π/2 rad. B1: equals speed of peg.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('1adc927f-d931-5126-abae-4e93b211d63e', '70f5e4f8-856a-42b5-a2db-b89d66bae49a', '4e983cb3-2f8d-40bb-bc3f-1575fb3c3a35', 'a-level', '18.3', '9702 coursebook 18.3 · Describing oscillations', 49, 49, 'A displacement–time graph shows two oscillations of the same frequency 25 Hz. Their peaks are separated by 12 ms. (a) Calculate the period. (b) Calculate the phase difference in radians. (c) Explain why a separation of 52 ms would give the same phase difference. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.885', 'rad', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) T = 1/f = 0.040 s = 40 ms. (b) φ = 2π × 12 ÷ 40 = 1.88 rad. (c) 52 ms = 40 + 12 ms: one extra whole period does not change the phase difference. Mark scheme (5 marks). A1: T = 40 ms. C1: φ = 2πΔt/T. A1: φ = 1.88 rad. B1: extra whole period. B1: phase repeats every 2π.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg9bcdb4a5-t fg9bcdb4a5-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg9bcdb4a5-t">Two 25 Hz oscillations</title><desc id="fg9bcdb4a5-d">Displacement against time for two 25 Hz oscillations (period 40 ms) whose peaks are 12 ms apart.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="77.7" y1="34" x2="77.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="113.5" y1="34" x2="113.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="203" y1="34" x2="203" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="292.5" y1="34" x2="292.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;str